const study = studies.find((s) => s.slug === "resource-capacity-2026-11-02");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / More of what keeps a ship running</h2>
    <p>
      The ship’s depletable reserve accounts for oxygen, food, lighting, heat,
      fuel and electricity. Sunlight and gravity replenish that same reserve.
      The world therefore permits some unexplained conversion between collected
      energy and the ship’s material and energy needs. “Energy” remains the UI
      label; neither the reserve nor the conversion needs a technical name yet.
      What is physically stored remains the central design question.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every ship carries 100 intrinsically. The normal fitting adds 85; the
      large adds 140 at twice the mass. These are givens, not balance proposals.
      Both must read as the same device, with form communicating the larger
      reserve and heavier construction. Neither generates resources. It is
      static, mounts above or below the hull, and needs no animation or sound.
    </p>
    <p>
      This study concerns the identity and expression of that extra storage.
      Basic equipment can contain extraordinary technology that is commonplace
      in this world. Its name, short part-list description and construction will
      develop from that premise, without requiring a scientific account of the
      conversion or a new name for the UI resource.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Established visual references</summary>
      <p>
        The production Wasp and Scout have narrow raised bodies and distinct
        projecting appendages; Fighter is a broad wedge, while Corvette is an
        elongated armored mass. Compact black canopies establish scale. Light
        and Heavy ion engines use closed faceted housings with blue outlets;
        fusion separates its masses with an exposed connecting gap; plasma has a
        broader chamber and layered outlet. Flat light-facing planes and dark
        sides give all of them depth.
      </p>
      <p>
        The integrated utility artwork uses cool bodies and bright structural
        attachments: storage’s cross-braces, solar’s side connections, gravity’s
        ring attachments and repair’s central bridge. The accepted family
        direction is #CEDB17 on connectors, struts and attachments, with
        different silhouettes allowed. The new device must retain that identity;
        its mechanism and silhouette remain open.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/lab/utility-artwork"
          >Integrated support equipment and all four production hulls</a
        >
        ·
        <a href="/lab/studies/utility-machinery-2026-11-02#reference"
          >Four ships and four engine references</a
        >
        ·
        <a href="/lab/studies/utility-machinery-2026-11-02#family-colour"
          >Accepted utility-family direction</a
        >
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>02 / Giving the reserve a physical identity</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>
        02.1 / Containing, differentiating or accumulating potential
      </summary>
      <p>
        Potential is a promising way to describe what is held: something the
        ship can draw on to meet different material and energy needs. It remains
        a fictional premise, not a defined substance. The storage fitting adds
        capacity; collection and conversion need not happen inside it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Potential Containment</strong>
        emphasizes holding an unusual state stable. A
        <strong>Potential Containment Unit</strong>
        sounds like an ordinary piece of engineering built around that task. It
        offers a reason for a chamber, boundaries and supporting structure,
        without yet selecting their appearance. “Containment” may suggest
        danger; instability and failure mechanics are not established.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Potential Differentiator</strong>
        suggests making a difference between states, or turning undifferentiated
        potential into something specific. This could explain how the ship
        obtains oxygen, heat or fuel, but it gives the device a processing role
        beyond the established capacity increase. It remains a useful
        world-building possibility, with a less direct connection to this
        fitting’s job.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Potential Accumulator</strong>
        emphasizes collecting and holding a reserve for later use. It makes
        additional capacity easy to understand and sounds like commonplace ship
        equipment. “Accumulation” here means accepting input from the ship’s
        collectors, not generating or harvesting resources independently. It
        leaves the physical storage mechanism open.
      </p>
      <p>
        The useful distinction is what the name foregrounds: the containment
        mechanism, the transformation, or the stored reserve. This comparison
        led to the subspace-well identity and the name selected in 02.2.
      </p>
      <h4>A subspace well as the storage mechanism</h4>
      <p>
        A proposed mechanism gives containment a specific meaning: manufactured
        equipment maintains a small subspace well holding convertible potential.
        Sunlight and gravity collection replenish the reserve; the ship draws on
        it to meet its material and energy needs. “Quantum potential” is a
        possible fictional term, not a settled name or a claim about real
        physics. The premise does not require the ship to manipulate information
        or holographic space generally.
      </p>
      <p>
        The visible part would be the machinery that anchors and contains the
        well. Its external size need not match the space inside. The normal and
        large versions could use the same construction, with the large unit
        carrying more substantial containment hardware for a greater reserve.
        This is a proposed explanation for their form and mass difference, not a
        new power draw, failure rule or gameplay effect.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Potential Well</strong>
        names what is contained; <strong>Well Containment Unit</strong> names
        the installed machinery; <strong>Potential Accumulator</strong> names
        its purpose. The mechanism makes these compatible descriptions of one
        object. The selected name and assembly principle are recorded in 02.2;
        the precise nature of the stored potential remains deliberately
        unspecified.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details id="name-description">
      <summary>02.2 / Potential Well</summary>
      <p>
        <strong>Selected name: Potential Well.</strong>
        The device consists of multiple well containment units. Together they
        hold a reserve of potential in subspace, available to the ship’s
        systems. Both sizes share this identity; the number, proportions and
        arrangement of containment units remain for appearance exploration.
      </p>
      <h4>Part-list description · approved</h4>
      <blockquote>
        A bank of well containment units stores potential in subspace, ready to
        supply the ship with matter and energy as needed.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        This description leaves the stored state mysterious while explaining its
        purpose. Matter and energy describe what the reserve supplies; no choice
        between stored matter, energy or quantum states is required. The name
        and multiple-unit construction are settled; the wording is approved.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details id="construction-ideas">
      <summary>02.3 / Nine constructions for a Potential Well</summary>
      <p>
        Text-only alternatives. Each device contains multiple well containment
        units. All use solid, flat-shaded construction, with #CEDB17 on
        structural connections and attachments. The production ships establish
        raised masses and restrained planes; the engines demonstrate enclosed
        housings, separated assemblies and layered construction. The utility
        family permits different silhouettes. None of the constructions below is
        selected.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each pair keeps the same architecture and orientation. The large device
        should look heavier and hold more, without implying exactly twice the
        capacity: it adds 140 against the normal device’s 85, at twice the mass.
        Unit counts are visual proposals, not capacity arithmetic. Every
        construction is static, uses a hull-facing mounting interface and must
        eventually be checked above and below real hulls at enlarged and flight
        scales.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Clamped stack.</strong>
          Several thick, faceted containment wafers sit along a short spine,
          compressed between substantial end plates. Dark seams distinguish the
          units; bright tie bars hold the assembly together. The large version
          lengthens the same stack and thickens its end plates. It suggests
          restrained pressure, with a risk of reading as a battery stack.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Well cassette.</strong>
          A squat armored cassette holds a row of containment cartridges whose
          capped ends remain visible in one recessed face. Bright side latches
          connect the cassette to its mount. The large version extends the row
          and deepens the housing. It feels like routine serviceable equipment;
          excessive enclosure could hide its multiple-unit identity.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ring bank.</strong>
          Thick polygonal collars sit side by side in a rigid carrier, each
          enclosing a dark central well. Bright bridges join the collars at
          their outer edges. The large version adds a collar and reinforces the
          same carrier. Repeated openings make containment legible, but the
          existing gravity device’s ring makes family-member confusion a
          concern.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Faceted cluster.</strong>
          Several compact polyhedral containment capsules nest around a central
          mounting boss. Their broad faces form a low, roughly hexagonal
          assembly, connected by bright short braces. The large version enlarges
          the capsules and boss while retaining their arrangement. It suggests
          separate pockets held together, though the silhouette could become
          lumpy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Paired combs.</strong>
          Two opposed structural rails carry inward-facing containment blocks,
          with a narrow dark channel between them. Bright crosspieces join the
          rails at both ends. The large version extends the rails with another
          matched pair of blocks and heavier crosspieces. Its repeated teeth
          imply modular machinery; thin teeth could disappear at flight scale.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Armored spine.</strong>
          A central structural keel carries a sequence of broad containment
          lobes on either side, like short ribs beneath a protective ridge.
          Bright root connections secure the lobes to the keel. The large
          version retains the same rib arrangement with deeper lobes and a
          heavier keel. It belongs naturally beside the ships’ raised bodies,
          but must remain recognizable as attached equipment rather than another
          tiny hull.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Nested cradles.</strong>
          Several U-shaped containment assemblies interlock around separate dark
          cores, creating a compact stepped block with visible gaps. Bright
          heels and connecting feet support the cradles. The large version
          deepens the same assemblies and widens their supports. It exposes how
          the units are held, though the negative spaces may merge when small.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Containment drum.</strong>
          A short, polygonal drum is divided into several wedge-shaped well
          units around a solid hub. Bright end clamps secure the segments; the
          face has broad radial seams, with no open central hole. The large
          version becomes a deeper, slightly wider drum with heavier clamps. It
          offers a dense single mass whose divisions reveal multiple units, but
          could resemble a rotating mechanism despite being static.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Split vault.</strong>
          Two heavy faceted shells enclose a bank of well units, leaving a
          narrow inspection gap that reveals their repeated caps. Bright
          transverse locks bridge the shells and connect to a mounting shoe. The
          large version lengthens the vault and thickens the shells while
          preserving the gap and lock arrangement. It gives containment a strong
          protective character; the hidden units must still read as a bank
          rather than one chamber.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The clamped stack makes repetition clearest; the well cassette makes the
        technology feel most ordinary; the split vault gives containment the
        strongest physical presence. These are reasons to compare them, not
        selections. No glowing contents, animation or sound is needed to explain
        any of the nine objects.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details id="visual-examples">
      <summary>02.4 / Nine top-down constructions</summary>
      <p>
        Orthographic top views: no perspective, tilted camera or displaced
        underside. Broad light-facing surfaces and dark opposing facets describe
        raised volumes. Circular collars and the segmented drum use radial
        construction; housings use corners where plates and bevels meet. The
        cool light and dark body palette comes from integrated utility artwork;
        deep recesses use the engines’ #12171e. Structural faces retain #CEDB17,
        with light and dark shading.
      </p>
      <p>
        Normal variants only, at exact 8× and 1× drawing scales, to compare the
        nine constructions without the distraction of size variants. At 1× the
        proposed bodies are roughly 18–28 pixels across; this is a study sizing
        assumption, not verified production mount size. The same SVG supplies
        both views. Large variants are deferred. Real hull mounting and 0.65
        flight-scale verification remain a later comparison.
      </p>
      <PotentialWellNine />
    </details>
    <details id="refinements">
      <summary>02.5 / Cassettes, rings and cradles</summary>
      <p>
        Well cassette, Ring bank and Nested cradles communicate the concept, but
        none is yet the chosen form. This round retains their multiple
        containment units and develops three constructions for each. Rows group
        the families; columns are alternatives, not size tiers. Normal variants
        only, each shown at 8× and 1×.
      </p>
      <p>
        A slot locates the attachment; it does not bound the device’s
        silhouette. Wider trays, longer banks and projecting cradles
        deliberately overhang their central mounting region. The proposed
        structures keep #CEDB17 on clamps, bridges and connecting feet. Rounded
        capsule surfaces use broad flat bands; rings and housings retain facets
        where those explain their construction.
      </p>
      <p>
        These remain orthographic top views. The larger footprints test presence
        and separation of the wells; fit, overlap and visibility above or below
        production hulls remain unverified. No new capacity, mass or operating
        behavior is implied.
      </p>
      <PotentialWellRefinements />
    </details>
    <details id="chambers">
      <summary>02.6 / Broad planes over underlying rails</summary>
      <p>
        Underlying rail supports are retained. The previous layered rims and
        beveled rails were rejected for looking artificially extruded. This
        replacement removes their stacked lips, inner-wall bands and clipped
        bevels.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each separate chamber uses four broad sloping planes around a dark
        recess. There are no shifted lower outlines or perspective faces. Yellow
        rails have a straight footprint and two longitudinal surfaces, without
        end bevels. Light comes from above and left.
      </p>
      <p>
        The nine alternatives compare square, diamond and elongated chamber
        bodies across transverse pairs, longitudinal pairs and groups of three.
        Normal devices only, at 8× and 1×. The question is whether these simpler
        solid forms belong beside the ships; their fit on real hulls is still
        unverified.
      </p>
      <PotentialWellChambers />
    </details>
    <details id="long-pairs">
      <summary>02.7 / Long pairs with the original wells</summary>
      <p>
        These combine the longitudinal arrangement and underlying rails of 02.6
        option 05 with the original simple ring wells from 02.4. The wells
        retain their single band of broad facets and dark centers; no extra rims
        or inner-wall bands are added. Three alternatives vary chamber size,
        spacing and rail separation. The yellow stays beneath the chambers.
      </p>
      <PotentialWellLongPairs />
    </details>
    <h3 id="sizing">02.8 / Close coupling on the fleet</h3>
    <p>
      02.7 option 03, Long pair / Close coupling, is the accepted normal form.
      This tool places its refined artwork at actual production equipment-mount
      coordinates on all four hulls. Light ion engines fill every engine mount
      and every available aft mount. The decided scale of 0.55 applies across
      ships; each ship’s equipment mount can be selected separately.
    </p>
    <p>
      Scale 0.55 is decided. Yellow rail extensions above and below the wells
      are removed; the two yellow connections remain between them, with a
      restrained grey lattice in the gap. Lighting now faces the ship’s
      upper-left light source. The lattice is a detail whose flight-scale
      visibility remains to be judged. Production layering is the default;
      above/below overrides are explicit comparison fixtures. The device may
      extend beyond its slot. This is a visual sizing tool, not a changed
      loadout contract or gameplay implementation.
    </p>
    <PotentialWellSizing />
    <h3 id="heavy">02.9 / A heavier Potential Well</h3>
    <p>
      The normal variant is accepted: Close coupling at 0.55, upper-left
      lighting, yellow connections only between wells, and a grey lattice
      between the connectors. This is the reference for the heavy device. The
      heavy adds 140 capacity and weighs twice as much; the normal adds 85. The
      heavier construction must remain recognizable as the same equipment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three proposed forms compare larger chambers, an additional chamber and
      broader chambers. All retain the normal’s palette, well construction and
      connector placement, with a slightly stronger lattice. The same 0.55
      artwork scale is applied to every example; the heavy forms gain actual
      dimensions rather than a different display zoom. A / Larger containment
      wells is selected; B and C remain comparison alternatives. Unit count and
      visible volume do not define new capacity arithmetic.
    </p>
    <PotentialWellHeavy />
    <p>
      <strong>Heavy selected: A / Larger containment wells.</strong>
      Two enlarged wells preserve the normal device’s construction and identity.
      Both sizes retain the inter-well yellow connectors, lattice and upper-left
      lighting. The heavy’s real-hull fit and flight-scale readability still
      need verification; selection settles its form, not those checks.
    </p>
  </section>
  <aside>
    <h2>Implementation / delivered locally</h2>
    <p>
      Normal and heavy Potential Wells now use shared procedural artwork in the
      catalog, builder, flight and Lab. Normal retains the battery save ID, adds
      85 capacity and weighs 18; heavy adds 140 and weighs 36. Intrinsic
      capacity stays 100. The approved description is in the part list. Existing
      normal saves remain compatible.
    </p>
    <p>
      The fleet comparison above now renders both production fittings, with a
      variant selector. Inspection covered every heavy equipment mount across
      the four ships. Production layering partially hides the Wasp fitting and
      conceals Scout’s below-hull fitting; these established rules are
      preserved. The lattice is subordinate at flight scale. This is local
      implementation, not a published release.
    </p>
    <p>
      Verification evidence:
      artifacts/verification/2026-11-02T19-09-02.924Z-zV7fNI/report.json.
      Contracts, commit checks, types, 119 unit tests, build, Firebase rules and
      competition passed. Browser checks passed 28 of 29; the remaining Scout
      test used the retired display name. After its selector was corrected, all
      three Scout checks passed; see artifacts/potential-well/scout-recheck.log.
      Full-tree lint reports pre-existing formatting issues in
      EnergyReserveForms.astro and energy-support-2026-11-02.astro, outside this
      cycle.
    </p>
  </aside>
  <section id="readiness">
    <h2>03 / Readiness: one device, two capacities</h2>
    <p>
      <strong
        >The design is ready for implementation, with heavy mounting
        verification included in delivery.</strong
      >
      The selected references are close-coupling-sized.svg for the normal and
      heavy-larger.svg for the heavy, in this study’s asset directory. Both use
      0.55 artwork scale, not the existing production utility scale of 0.35.
      Their SVG view boxes differ; conversion must preserve their coordinate
      dimensions rather than normalize their bounding boxes to equal size.
    </p>
    <p>
      The normal’s outer dimensions are approximately 7.7 × 16.5 ship units; the
      heavy’s are 9.9 × 20.9. Both have two wells, yellow connections only
      between them and a grey lattice. Preserve the selected simple planes,
      upper-left lighting and static construction. There are no animation,
      sound, collector or conversion mechanics in this delivery.
    </p>
    <p>
      Production already has shared procedural utility faces, a utility
      renderer, equipment mounts, hull layering, catalog entries and loadout
      validation. The battery renderer currently selects the placeholder storage
      artwork. These systems can carry the selected drawings without
      dependencies or changes to ship geometry. Convert the SVG paths and
      recessed centers into the shared drawing representation; express lattice
      strokes as filled geometry where required by its polygon contract. Reuse
      those definitions in the Lab and game.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Pre-implementation baseline:</strong>
      the battery ID was named Energy storage, had mass 18 and added 100
      capacity. Intrinsic capacity is already 100. There was no heavy storage
      ID. Delivery must explicitly implement +85 normal and +140 heavy, with
      heavy mass twice normal. Retaining existing normal mass 18 and assigning
      heavy mass 36 is the smallest implementation proposal; the study settles
      the ratio, not a newly chosen absolute mass. Keep the UI resource label
      energy.
    </p>
    <p>
      Retain the existing battery ID for saved normal fittings while changing
      its display name to Potential Well. Add a heavy ID through the Zod
      contracts before catalog or simulation changes; proposed display name:
      Heavy Potential Well. The approved description is “A bank of well
      containment units stores potential in subspace, ready to supply the ship
      with matter and energy as needed.” Make it available to the part list
      without inventing a resource-conversion system.
    </p>
    <p>
      Normal mounting has been reviewed with real hull artwork at the selected
      size. Heavy mounting remains an explicit verification gap: inspect all
      equipment positions, above/below occlusion, neighboring Light ion engines
      and aft fittings on all four ships at enlarged and 0.65 flight scale. Fine
      lattice visibility may be lost at flight scale; identification must
      survive through the two wells and overall size. Saved builds and stock
      ships using batteries will receive the new +85 effect, so their displayed
      capacity and endurance must be checked. No mass, balance or art changes
      beyond this scope are implied.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="scope">
    <h2>04 / Scope: shared artwork, then functional fittings</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>No change to docs/design.md is proposed.</strong>
      The feature decisions belong in the ship-builder decision record
      (docs/history/ship-builder.md), equipment contracts and shared artwork.
      Both normal and heavy visual directions are decided. The delivery scope
      was accepted and implementation authorized; publishing remains a separate
      action.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Shared drawings and mounting evidence — small to medium.</strong
        >
        Add the two selected procedural drawings at their decided dimensions,
        preserving the study references. Extend the Lab to render both from
        shared definitions on every production hull with Light ions in engine
        and available aft mounts. Verify orientation, lighting, occlusion,
        footprint and flight-scale distinction. This slice can deliver artwork
        and mounting evidence independently of catalog changes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Equipment contracts, catalog and capacity — medium; follows slice
          1.</strong
        >
        Keep battery compatibility for normal fittings, add the heavy ID and
        capacity data through Zod, implement +85/+140 and the 2:1 mass ratio,
        and connect each fitting to the correct shared drawing. Add the chosen
        name and approved description to the part list. Update
        docs/history/ship-builder.md to distinguish delivered behavior from
        historical battery rules. Verify old normal loadouts still parse, heavy
        loadouts round-trip, slot compatibility holds, intrinsic capacity stays
        100, and combinations yield correct mass and capacity.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Integrated verification — small; follows slice 2.</strong>
        Check builder and flight on all four ships, including full initial
        reserve, depletion, stock loadouts and derived endurance. Run required
        repository gates and retain their results. Review the selected heavy
        artwork on actual mounts separately from automated correctness. Repair
        integration defects without silently changing the accepted size or
        construction.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Implementation can preserve normal mass 18 and use heavy mass 36 unless a
      different absolute mass is chosen. Heavy Potential Well is a proposed
      catalog label, not an additional lore decision. The heavy mount review is
      a remaining delivery check; it is not evidence that the selected form has
      failed. No release, deployment, new resource terminology or additional
      utility behavior is included.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>
